WEBVTT

00:00.660 --> 00:02.500
What I want to go
over in this video

00:02.500 --> 00:08.029
is a special case
of L'Hopital's Rule.

00:08.029 --> 00:10.031
And it's a more
constrained version

00:10.031 --> 00:11.780
of the general case
we've been looking at.

00:11.780 --> 00:13.930
But it's still very powerful
and very applicable.

00:13.930 --> 00:17.060
And the reason why we're going
to go over this special case

00:17.060 --> 00:20.180
is because its proof is
fairly straightforward

00:20.180 --> 00:24.100
and will give you an intuition
for why L'Hopital's Rule works

00:24.100 --> 00:25.100
at all.

00:25.100 --> 00:27.070
So the special case
of L'Hopital's Rule

00:27.070 --> 00:31.510
is a situation where
f of a is equal to 0.

00:31.510 --> 00:33.520
f prime of a exists.

00:36.280 --> 00:40.780
g of a is equal to 0.

00:40.780 --> 00:43.890
g prime of a exists.

00:43.890 --> 00:49.600
If these constraints
are met, then the limit,

00:49.600 --> 00:59.810
as x approaches a of
f of x over g of x,

00:59.810 --> 01:10.150
is going to be equal to f
prime of a over g prime of a.

01:10.150 --> 01:11.870
So it's very similar
to the general case.

01:11.870 --> 01:13.330
It's little bit
more constrained.

01:13.330 --> 01:15.021
We're assuming that
f prime of a exists.

01:15.021 --> 01:16.520
We're not just
taking the limit now.

01:16.520 --> 01:19.740
We're assuming f prime of a and
g prime of a actually exist.

01:19.740 --> 01:23.490
But notice if we substitute
a right over here we get 0/0.

01:23.490 --> 01:24.940
But that if the
derivatives exist

01:24.940 --> 01:27.390
we could just evaluate
the derivatives at a,

01:27.390 --> 01:28.500
and then we get the limit.

01:28.500 --> 01:30.480
So this is very close
to the general case

01:30.480 --> 01:32.140
of L'Hopital's Rule.

01:32.140 --> 01:34.200
Now let's actually prove it.

01:34.200 --> 01:37.210
And to prove it, we're going
to start with the right hand

01:37.210 --> 01:40.950
and then show that if we use
the definition of derivatives,

01:40.950 --> 01:43.390
we get the left hand
right over here.

01:43.390 --> 01:44.810
So let me do that.

01:44.810 --> 01:46.280
So I'll do it right over here.

01:46.280 --> 01:50.830
So f prime of a
is equal to what,

01:50.830 --> 01:53.660
by the definition
of derivatives?

01:53.660 --> 01:55.550
Well, we could view
that as the limit

01:55.550 --> 02:05.440
as x approaches a of f of x
minus f of a over x minus a.

02:05.440 --> 02:08.300
So this is literally just
a slope between two points.

02:08.300 --> 02:11.940
So like, if you have your
function f of x like this,

02:11.940 --> 02:17.500
this is the point a, f
of a right over here.

02:17.500 --> 02:23.000
This right over here
is the point x, f of x.

02:23.000 --> 02:24.600
This expression
right over here is

02:24.600 --> 02:26.300
the slope between
these two points.

02:26.300 --> 02:30.050
The change in our y value
is f of x minus f of a.

02:30.050 --> 02:32.970
The change in our f
value is x minus a.

02:32.970 --> 02:35.860
So this expression is just
the slope of this line.

02:35.860 --> 02:38.000
And we're just taking
the-- let me actually

02:38.000 --> 02:40.470
do that in a different
color-- the line that

02:40.470 --> 02:43.475
connects these two points,
that's the slope of it.

02:43.475 --> 02:45.060
I'll do that in white.

02:45.060 --> 02:47.400
The slope of the line that
connects those two points.

02:47.400 --> 02:49.130
And we're taking
the limit as x gets

02:49.130 --> 02:50.547
closer and closer
and closer to a.

02:50.547 --> 02:52.129
So this is just
another way of writing

02:52.129 --> 02:53.540
the definition of
the derivative.

02:53.540 --> 02:54.640
So that's fine.

02:54.640 --> 02:56.370
Let's do the same
thing for g prime of a.

02:56.370 --> 02:59.410
So f prime of a
over g prime of a,

02:59.410 --> 03:01.480
is going to be this
business which is in orange,

03:01.480 --> 03:03.950
f prime of a over g prime of a.

03:03.950 --> 03:07.400
Which we can write as
the limit as x approaches

03:07.400 --> 03:14.944
a of g of x minus g
of a over x minus a.

03:14.944 --> 03:16.860
Well, in the numerator,
we're taking the limit

03:16.860 --> 03:18.300
as x approaches a, and
in the denominator,

03:18.300 --> 03:20.230
we're taking the limit
as x approaches a.

03:20.230 --> 03:21.820
So we can just rewrite this.

03:21.820 --> 03:25.880
This we can rewrite as
the limit as x approaches

03:25.880 --> 03:28.600
a of all this
business in orange.

03:28.600 --> 03:33.990
f of x minus f of
a, over x minus a,

03:33.990 --> 03:38.600
over all the business in green.

03:38.600 --> 03:44.375
g of x minus g of a, all
of that over x minus a.

03:44.375 --> 03:47.170
Now, to simplify this, we
can multiply the numerator

03:47.170 --> 03:49.960
and the denominator by x minus
a to get rid of these x minus

03:49.960 --> 03:50.460
a's.

03:50.460 --> 03:51.260
So let's do that.

03:51.260 --> 03:56.980
Let's multiply by x
minus a over x minus a.

03:56.980 --> 03:59.740
So the numerator, x minus a,
and we're dividing by x minus a.

03:59.740 --> 04:00.970
Those cancel out.

04:00.970 --> 04:02.650
And then these two cancel out.

04:02.650 --> 04:07.430
And we're left with this thing
over here is equal to the limit

04:07.430 --> 04:11.530
as x approaches a
of, in the numerator

04:11.530 --> 04:14.610
we have f of x minus f of a.

04:14.610 --> 04:20.970
And in the denominator, we
have g of x minus g of a.

04:20.970 --> 04:23.630
And I think you see
where this is going.

04:23.630 --> 04:25.330
What is f(a) equal to?

04:25.330 --> 04:27.480
Well, we assumed f
of a is equal to 0.

04:27.480 --> 04:29.580
That's why we're
using L'Hopital's Rule

04:29.580 --> 04:33.030
from the get go. f of a is equal
to 0, g of a is equal to 0.

04:33.030 --> 04:36.170
f of a is equal to 0.
g of a is equal to 0.

04:36.170 --> 04:42.340
And this simplifies to the
limit as x approaches a of f

04:42.340 --> 04:47.290
prime of x, sorry of f of
x, we've got to be careful.

04:47.290 --> 04:52.720
Of f of x over g of x.

04:52.720 --> 04:57.390
So we just showed that if f of
a equals 0, g of a equals 0,

04:57.390 --> 05:00.405
and these two derivatives exist,
then the derivatives evaluated

05:00.405 --> 05:03.420
at a over each other are
going to be equal to the limit

05:03.420 --> 05:06.450
as x approaches a of
f of x over g of x.

05:06.450 --> 05:08.973
Or the limit as x
approaches a of f

05:08.973 --> 05:12.570
of x over g of x is going to
be equal to f prime of a over g

05:12.570 --> 05:13.130
prime of a.

05:13.130 --> 05:16.480
So fairly straightforward
proof for the special case--

05:16.480 --> 05:18.860
the special case, not
the more general case--

05:18.860 --> 05:21.310
of L'Hopital's Rule.
